bound-lock/1 about="head reads an API by the document its provider already publishes. Here is one way for each level of access: the first two run against a real public API and its real document, and the third as head does it for any document that declares a key.\n\n### No access: Open-Meteo\n\nOpen-Meteo's forecast needs no key, but its document ([forecast.yml](https://github.com/open-meteo/open-meteo/blob/main/openapi/forecast.yml)) does not say so, and head never guesses: an operation whose document says nothing about access is listed and never called. One overlay says what the provider says on its own site:\n\n```yaml\noverlay: 1.0.0\ninfo: { title: Open-Meteo forecast, read without a key, version: '1' }\nactions:\n  - target: \"$.paths['/v1/forecast'].get\"\n    description: the free forecast needs no key, as open-meteo.com says\n    update: { security: [] }\n```\n\nConnect `forecast.yml` as `weather` with that overlay beside it and read `get-v1-forecast` at a latitude and a longitude. The document names two servers, so the person picks one, once. The answer comes back typed by what the document declares, one value a line, with a signed receipt of the read (abridged):\n\n```text\nanswer/286b453612143d46/current/temperature_2m        number  11.9\nanswer/286b453612143d46/current_units/temperature_2m  text    °C\nanswer/286b453612143d46/current/wind_speed_10m        number  7.9\nread/weather/get-v1-forecast/286b453612143d46          digest  sha256:… signed by head\n```\n\n### A declared flow: Spotify\n\nSpotify's document ([the community OpenAPI description](https://github.com/sonallux/spotify-web-api)) declares OAuth 2.0 with an authorization code. head compiles it into the two steps it takes, the provider's page with PKCE and then the token exchange, and nobody writes a line of it. What it needs is yours: the client id of an app you registered with Spotify, signed into the place as `keys/access/music/oauth_2_0 client <id>`. Until then head says so and calls nothing.\n\n### A secret entered by hand\n\nWhen a document declares a bearer token or an API key and no way to obtain one, the key is a secret input. head asks for it on its own one-time page, never through the model, and only when the place's root allows a secret with `access/secret-inputs`. It is kept in the keychain, never in a file.\n\n### When the document says too little\n\nA login of its own (an email, a one-time code, a choice of account) is written once as an `x-access` flow, an Arazzo profile in an overlay, and every API of the same issuer refers to it with one `$ref`. The profile and its vectors are on topos-openapi's own page, `docs/x-access.md` in `@lapxo/topos-openapi`. Every API connected this way is read the same way afterwards: one access per issuer, each answer typed and kept." at=policy:head/docs by=target form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/page/connect-any-api value=lock
